# Line endings are pinned deliberately, not for tidiness.
#
# The modularization of WindowTweaks.ahk is verified by a "motion proof": the
# resolved #Include stream is sorted and hashed, and a pure code-motion commit
# must not change that hash (scripts\Check-Split.ps1). With core.autocrlf=true
# and no rules here, files round-trip between LF and CRLF depending on which
# tool last wrote them - src\RenderCore.ahk was already LF in the working tree
# while every other .ahk was CRLF - and the hash then differs for reasons that
# have nothing to do with the code. Pinning makes checkout deterministic.
#
# This repository holds two independent implementations. The Windows one
# (AutoHotkey v2, PowerShell, WinForms setup) is native CRLF. The Linux port in
# linux/ is read by bash, cmake and g++ on a Unix host, so it is native LF and
# is overridden at the bottom of this file. That override is not cosmetic:
# install.sh checked out with CRLF fails on Linux with
#   bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M
# and it fails only when someone actually runs it on Linux, never on the
# machine that committed it.

*.ahk   text eol=crlf
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
*.cs    text eol=crlf
*.md    text eol=crlf
*.json  text eol=crlf
*.rsp   text eol=crlf

# Unix-native regardless of where they live.
*.sh    text eol=lf

# Binary - never touch, never diff.
*.ico   binary
*.exe   binary
*.png   binary

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Linux port - everything under linux/ is LF. These rules come last so they
# win over the CRLF defaults above for *.md and *.json.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
linux/**            text eol=lf
linux/**/*.md       text eol=lf
linux/**/*.json     text eol=lf
linux/CMakeLists.txt text eol=lf
